new_candidates.body:
The concurrent-halt guard in `Finally` from #1229 only protects `FinallyNode::halt()` itself. `Tree::haltTree()` halts the root and then calls `haltNode()` on every node through `applyRecursiveVisitor` ([bt_factory.cpp#L680-L684](https://github.com/BehaviorTree/BehaviorTree.CPP/blob/365b2e49/src/bt_factory.cpp#L680-L684)). When a second thread calls `haltTree()` while the tick thread is in the `halt_timeout_msec` loop, the guard makes the second `Finally::halt()` return right away, but the sweep still reaches the cleanup child. A `StatefulActionNode` cleanup then gets `onHalted()` while its `onRunning()` is still executing on the tick thread.

The `Finally` docs describe this case as supported ([finally_node.h#L40-L46](https://github.com/BehaviorTree/BehaviorTree.CPP/blob/365b2e49/include/behaviortree_cpp/controls/finally_node.h#L40-L46), [finally_node.cpp#L86-L96](https://github.com/BehaviorTree/BehaviorTree.CPP/blob/365b2e49/src/controls/finally_node.cpp#L86-L96)). They say a service callback can call `Tree::haltTree()` and that cleanup is never ticked concurrently.

To reproduce on `master` (365b2e49):

```cpp
#include "behaviortree_cpp/bt_factory.h"

#include <atomic>
#include <future>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

std::atomic<bool> in_on_running{ false };
std::atomic<int> overlaps{ 0 };
std::promise<void> entered;
std::shared_future<void> release_cleanup;

// Needs two ticks; the second one (onRunning) blocks until released.
class SlowCleanup : public BT::StatefulActionNode
{
public:
  using BT::StatefulActionNode::StatefulActionNode;
  static BT::PortsList providedPorts() { return {}; }
  BT::NodeStatus onStart() override { return BT::NodeStatus::RUNNING; }
  BT::NodeStatus onRunning() override
  {
    in_on_running = true;
    entered.set_value();
    release_cleanup.wait();
    in_on_running = false;
    return BT::NodeStatus::SUCCESS;
  }
  void onHalted() override
  {
    if(in_on_running)
    {
      ++overlaps;
    }
  }
};

int main()
{
  BT::BehaviorTreeFactory factory;
  factory.registerNodeType<SlowCleanup>("SlowCleanup");
  factory.registerSimpleCondition("AlwaysRunning",
                                  [](BT::TreeNode&) { return BT::NodeStatus::RUNNING; });
  auto tree = factory.createTreeFromText(R"(
    <root BTCPP_format="4"><BehaviorTree>
      <Finally halt_timeout_msec="5000"><AlwaysRunning/><SlowCleanup/></Finally>
    </BehaviorTree></root>)");

  std::promise<void> release;
  release_cleanup = release.get_future().share();
  // The tick thread also halts, so Finally runs its halt_timeout_msec loop.
  std::thread tick_thread([&tree] {
    tree.tickOnce();
    tree.haltTree();
  });
  entered.get_future().wait();  // the loop is now inside onRunning()
  tree.haltTree();              // second halt, as from a service callback
  release.set_value();
  tick_thread.join();
  std::cout << "onHalted() while onRunning() was active: " << overlaps << "\n";
}
```

This prints `1` in every run (5 of 5). If the second thread calls `tree.rootNode()->haltNode()` instead of `tree.haltTree()`, it prints `0` (3 of 3), so the recursive sweep is what bypasses the guard.

I'm not sure the fix belongs in `Finally`, because the gap is in how `Tree::haltTree()` treats a node whose halt is still in progress. I tried making the sweep skip the subtree of a `Finally` that is halting. That opens another ordering: a parent's `resetChildren()` can reset the `Finally` to IDLE before the first halt reads its status, and then cleanup never runs. If a real fix is too big for now, a smaller step is to say in the docs that `Tree::haltTree()` must not be called while another halt of the same tree is running. Other suggestions are welcome.
